  "description": "Select an ordered list of multiple columns from a pandas DataFrame while preserving row ordering.\n\nMulti-column selection projects high-dimensional tables onto relevant feature subsets for modeling.\n\n### Mathematical Formulation\n$$\n\\text{sub\\_df} = \\text{df}[[c_1, c_2, \\dots, c_k]]\n$$\n\n### Step-by-Step Implementation\n1. Construct DataFrame from records list\n2. Select multiple specified column names in exact given order\n3. Export filtered DataFrame as record dictionaries\n\n### Returns\nList of records containing only the designated columns.",
  "theory": "## Understanding the Theory: Multi-Column Selection\n\n### Core Intuition: Why Do We Need This?\nReal-world tabular datasets are frequently messy, containing missing values, duplicates, and unaligned columns. Pandas provides powerful DataFrame primitives for reshaping, filtering, and cleaning structured data before training machine learning models. **Multi-Column Selection** performs structured data manipulation efficiently.\n\nMulti-column selection projects high-dimensional tables onto relevant feature subsets for modeling. In this problem, the goal is to: select an ordered list of multiple columns from a pandas dataframe while preserving row ordering.\n\n### Mathematical Principles & Formulation\nThe core governing equation for this operation is:\n\n$$\n\\text{sub\\_df} = \\text{df}[[c_1, c_2, \\dots, c_k]]\n$$\n\nEvery term in this formulation plays a precise mathematical role in ensuring dimensional consistency, numerical stability, and correct algorithmic behavior.\n\n### Step-by-Step Computational Workflow\n1. **Phase 1**: Construct DataFrame from records list\n2. **Phase 2**: Select multiple specified column names in exact given order\n3. **Phase 3**: Export filtered DataFrame as record dictionaries\n\n### Practical Machine Learning Applications & Nuances\n* **Vectorized Array Operations**: Applying vectorized transformations operates on contiguous C memory buffers under the hood, running orders of magnitude faster than iterating with Python loops.\n* **Data Cleansing**: Handling duplicates and missing data prevents bias and data leakage into downstream machine learning models.\n* **Memory Management**: Choosing appropriate column dtypes (e.g. categorical or int32 instead of object) drastically reduces DataFrame memory usage.",
  "examples": [
    "Input: records = [{\"id\": 1, \"name\": \"A\", \"val\": 10, \"score\": 90}, {\"id\": 2, \"name\": \"B\", \"val\": 20, \"score\": 85}], selected_columns = [\"name\", \"score\"]\n\nOutput: [{\"name\": \"A\", \"score\": 90}, {\"name\": \"B\", \"score\": 85}]\n\nExplanation: Evaluated pandas_multi_column_selection under standard test conditions."
  ],
  "requirements": "* Function must be named `pandas_multi_column_selection`\n* Return value must match the exact expected structure and types\n* Handle edge cases cleanly and maintain numerical stability\n* Must run efficiently without redundant memory allocations",
